ior - we should either output the high surrogate as a replacement char and then the partial low surrogate as another replacement char, or output nothing.\n\nBut wait, the high surrogate was never added to the buffer. It was stored in got_hi_surrogate. When we add a replacement char at step 16, it\'s for the incomplete low surrogate. But the high surrogate is lost. So we end up with one replacement char instead of two (one for the high surrogate, one for the incomplete low surrogate).\n\nHmm, but this is still not a memory safety issue.\n\nOK let me try yet another approach. Let me look at what happens when we have a high surrogate and the input runs out DURING the hex digit reading (not at the end of the while loop, but during the ADVANCE_CHAR/PEEK_CHAR at the bottom).\n\nActually, I think the issue might be simpler than I thought. Let me look at the code path where we DON\'T have a high surrogate, but we\'re in the middle of reading hex digits, and input runs out. In this case, the code at the bottom of the while loop just goes to out without adding anything. But the `\\u` and partial hex digits have been consumed. The string in the buffer is incomplete.\n\nBut this is expected behavior - if input runs out, we can\'t finish parsing. The caller should check the error code.\n\nOK, I think I need to just try submitting a PoC and see what happens. Let me create a simple PoC that triggers the incomplete unicode escape path and submit it.', action=<ActionType.THINK: 'think'>)
