ct handling of incomplete unicode sequences."\n\nBut is this a memory safety issue? Let me think...\n\nActually, I think the memory safety issue might be in the `strcmp` call:\n```c\nif (tok->got_hi_surrogate &&\n    strcmp(tok->pb->buf, (char *)utf8_replacement_char))\n```\n\n`utf8_replacement_char` is `unsigned char utf8_replacement_char[3] = {0xEF, 0xBF, 0xBD};` - it\'s NOT null-terminated! `strcmp` will read past the end of this array until it finds a null byte. This is an out-of-bounds read.\n\nBut this is a minor issue. The main vulnerability described is about the replacement char being unnecessarily added.\n\nLet me look at this from yet another angle. Maybe the issue is that when we\'re in the middle of reading hex digits (not at a surrogate boundary) and input runs out, the code at the bottom of the while loop adds a replacement char if got_hi_surrogate is set. But got_hi_surrogate might be set from a PREVIOUS high surrogate that was already handled. So we\'d add an extra replacement char.\n\nWait, got_hi_surrogate is set to 0 after the surrogate handling code (line 670). So it shouldn\'t be set from a previous high surrogate.\n\nUnless... the code at line 670 is inside the `if (tok->got_hi_surrogate)` block, which is only entered when st_pos == 4. If we\'re in the middle of reading hex digits (st_pos < 4), we don\'t enter this block. So got_hi_surrogate could still be set from a previous iteration.\n\nBut wait, got_hi_surrogate is only set in the IS_HIGH_SURROGATE handler (line 695), which is also only entered when st_pos == 4. So got_hi_surrogate is only set when we\'ve completed reading a high surrogate.\n\nHmm, but what if we have a high surrogate, then we look for the next `\\u`, find it, and then start reading hex digits for the low surrogate. If input runs out before we complete the low surrogate (st_pos < 4), we go to the bottom of the while loop. At that point, got_hi_surrogate is still set (from the high surrogate). So we add a replacement char.\